    "file": "backend/app/services/search_service.py",
    "line": 131,
    "summary": "The checks branch re-fetches the Suite rows it already joined (a 9th query per keystroke): the page selects `Suite.name` only, then `select(Suite).where(Suite.id.in_(check_suites))` loads the same suites again for `effective_permissions`.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every search with ≥1 check hit costs an extra round-trip. Select the `Suite` entity in the page query and pass those objects on; fetching `limit + 1` in `_page` instead of a separate COUNT halves the 4 COUNT + 4 page queries while keeping `truncated` exact."
  },
  {
    "file": "frontend/src/components/shared/CommandPalette.tsx",
    "line": 117,
    "summary": "`onQueryChange` flips to `loading` and bumps `requestSeq` (aborting the in-flight request) on every raw keystroke even when the trimmed term is unchanged — a trailing space or a same-text paste blanks the results and re-issues the identical query.",
    "failure_scenario": "Results for `orders` shown; user types a space → list disappears, 'Searching…', the same `GET /search?q=orders` fires again after 200 ms, results reappear. Key the fetch and the reset on the trimmed term."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/services/search_service.py",
    "line": 23,
    "summary": "A second module now imports the leading-underscore `user_service._escape_like`, and the `ilike(pattern, escape=\"\\\\\")` pairing is repeated at six call sites — the LIKE-escaping rule has no shared home.",
    "failure_scenario": "`user_service` can change its private helper without knowing search depends on it; a mismatch between the escaper's backslash and a call site's `escape=` silently turns a literal `%` back into a wildcard. Promote to a shared `contains_ci(column, term)` / `starts_with_ci(column, term)` helper used by both."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/app/api/v1/search.py",
    "line": 88,
    "summary": "`search()` takes `include_all` and `is_admin`, which the only caller always sets to the same value, and `effective_permissions` then re-resolves the workspace role with its own DB query — one fact resolved three ways.",
    "failure_scenario": "A future caller passing `include_all=True, is_admin=False` gets every suite visible but non-admin connection hrefs; nothing in the signature says the two must agree. Collapse to one `is_admin` flag and derive `include_all`."
  },
  {
    "file": "backend/tests/api/test_search.py",
    "line": 126,
    "summary": "No test covers the ADR 0033 Viewer clamp at this boundary: a `viewer`-role user with an `edit` share must get the suite href, not `/checks/{id}/edit`; the behaviour rests on `cap_for_viewer` inside `effective_permissions`, untested here.",
    "failure_scenario": "If the service ever reads `Share.permission` directly (e.g. to drop the extra suite query above), a Viewer with an edit share is sent to an editor route that 403s, and this suite stays green. Add the `as_role(\"viewer\")` + `edit` share case asserting the suite href."
  }
]
```

Verified-benign while tracing (no comment posted): the viewer clamp and workspace-admin paths are correct today via `effective_permissions` (`cap_for_viewer`, `ADMIN` short-circuit); `accessible_suite_filter` / `accessible_suite_ids` scope checks and runs exactly like their list endpoints; connections are unscoped exactly as `GET /connections` is; all three hrefs (`/suites/:id/checks/:id/edit`, `/connections/:id/edit` behind `RequireRole admin`, `/results/:runId`) exist in `App.tsx`; the hex-only id-prefix term contains no LIKE metacharacters so the unescaped `like()` is safe; `focusable.focusTriggerAfterClose` is the current antd 6 prop; no new `<Route>` was added, so `e2e-demo/routes.ts` is unaffected; the `openapi.json` + generated client drift check passed in CI.

One thing outside the diff worth knowing: the PR's CI run has **"Frontend — dependency audit (pnpm audit)" failing** while the latest `main` run is green and this PR touches no `package.json`/lockfile, so it looks like a fresh advisory landed between the two runs rather than anything this branch introduced; the job log was not retrievable via `gh api`, so check it in the Actions UI before merging. The other backend/frontend jobs were still pending at review time.

